Growth in current in $L R_{2}$ branch when switch is closed is given by



$\begin{array}{l}$i=$\frac{E}{R_{2}}\left[$1-e^{-R_{2} t / L}\right] \\

\Rightarrow $\frac{d i}{d t}=\frac{E}{R_{2}} \cdot \frac{R_{2}}{L}$e^{-R_{2} t / L}=$\frac{E}{L}$e^{-$\frac{R_{2} t}{L}$}

\end{array}



Hence, potential drop across $\mathbf{L}=\left(\frac{E}{L} e^{-R_{2} t / L}\right) L=E e^{-R_{2} t / L}=12 e^{-\frac{2 t}{400 \times 10^{-3}}}=12 \mathrme^{-5 t} \mathrm{~V}$
